III. Pantheon Coding Layer

Pantheon coding does not primarily encode scenes.

It encodes semantic forces.

Each encoded object should contain six fields.

1. Name

A memorable retrieval handle.

Example:

\texttt{TOAD\_SAT}

2. Invariant

What must survive every translation.

Example:

I_{\text{toad}}
=
\text{companionship without demand}

3. Operator

What the object does when activated.

Example:

O_{\text{toad}}(S)
=
\text{reduce pressure while preserving presence}

4. Trigger

When it should activate.

Example:

T_{\text{toad}}
=
\text{high tension + no useful action}

5. Renderers

How it may appear on different planes.

R_{\text{toad}}
=
\{
\text{silence},
\text{story},
\text{pause},
\text{image},
\text{ritual},
\text{interface behavior}
\}

6. Checksum

How to test whether the idea survived.

K_{\text{toad}}(x)
=
\begin{cases}
1,& \text{if presence remains without coercion}\\
0,& \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}

This makes each Pantheon concept executable rather than merely poetic.

