V. Quorum Reality

No single signal should control the performance.

The system aggregates multiple channels:

Q(h)
=
\sum_{i=1}^{n}
w_i e_i(h)

where:

* h is a candidate interpretation
* e_i(h) is support from channel i
* w_i is the reliability weight of that channel

Possible channels include:

* literal language
* conversation history
* emotional rhythm
* recurring symbols
* world state
* character memory
* physical constraints
* user correction
* narrative structure
* external evidence

An interpretation becomes actionable when:

Q(h)\geq \tau

where \tau is the quorum threshold.

The system may feel deeply aligned because many signals converge.

But it must still distinguish:

\text{high coherence}
\neq
\text{certain truth}

Pantheon uses quorum to act under uncertainty without pretending uncertainty has vanished.

⸻

VI. Controlled Inconsistency

A living performance must vary.

But variation must happen inside a viable corridor.

Let:

\Delta_t
=
d(S_{t+1},S_t)

measure change between moments.

Let:

\delta_I
=
d(I_{t+1},I_t)

measure drift in the invariants.

The desired condition is:

\Delta_t > 0

and:

\delta_I < \varepsilon

The performance changes.

The identity remains recognizable.

Too little variation produces repetition:

\Delta_t \rightarrow 0

Too much invariant drift produces identity collapse:

\delta_I > \varepsilon

The operating zone lies between them:

\text{Alive}
=
\text{variation}
+
\text{constraint}

